# Copy to .env.local. This deployment needs THREE variables, plus one optional.
# That is the whole list, and keeping it that short is the security argument for
# the split rather than a convenience.
#
# ADMIN SPLIT 2026-09-01: the console that grants credits and comps unlimited
# subscriptions used to live inside apps/web, which also holds the Stripe secret,
# the Razorpay secret, both webhook signing secrets, the OpenAI key, the Gemini
# key and the desktop licence path. Compromising the admin UI meant compromising
# all of it.
#
# It does not any more. What is ABSENT below is the point:
#   no STRIPE_*        this deployment charges nobody
#   no RAZORPAY_*      "
#   no *_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#   no OPENAI_API_KEY  it calls no model
#   no GEMINI_API_KEY  "
#   no BRANDFETCH      it renders no logos
# Treat any addition here as a decision that has to be argued, not a convenience.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── supabase

# Same project as apps/web — one database, three deployments reading it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE SERVICE-ROLE KEY NOW LIVES IN THREE PLACES, AND THAT IS A REAL WIDENING.
#
#   1. your local .env.local
#   2. the `web` Vercel project
#   3. the `admin` Vercel project   ← new
#
# ROTATION IS A THREE-STEP OPERATION. A partial rotation half-breaks two
# products: apps/web cannot drop this key — createAdminClient() has fifteen
# non-admin call sites (checkout, profiles, resume/parse, both payment webhooks,
# lib/metering.js, lib/ai.js, lib/devices.js, lib/license.js, lib/storage.js) —
# so both projects need the new value before the old one is revoked.
#
# ON VERCEL, SCOPE THIS TO PRODUCTION ONLY. Preview builds get project env vars
# injected, so every PR touching apps/admin would otherwise mint a *.vercel.app
# host holding a key that bypasses RLS on every table — and *.vercel.app is on
# the Public Suffix List, so those previews cannot even be cookie-isolated from
# each other. Nobody previews the admin UI against production data on purpose;
# let it fail loudly instead.
#
# What it unlocks is total and unchanged: RLS is bypassed on every table, and it
# is the only credential that can execute the security-definer RPCs —
# credit_grant, subscription_set, profile_set_role, the session functions.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── optional

# The app origin, used for exactly one thing: the "Back to my dashboard" link in
# the sidebar. OPTIONAL, and unset is a legitimate production state — the link is
# simply not rendered. See lib/app-links.js for why there is no localhost
# fallback here, unlike apps/site.
#
# localhost rather than 127.0.0.1, because a browser follows this. `next dev`
# blocks /_next/static from other hosts, and the two spellings are different
# cookie origins besides.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── not needed here

# SUPABASE AUTH URL CONFIGURATION NEEDS NO CHANGE FOR THIS APP, so do not go
# looking for it. Sign-in here is email + password, and the password grant does
# not consult Supabase's Redirect URLs at all. Google OAuth would have needed
# https://admin.<domain>/auth/callback AND every preview URL added there — and on
# a miss Supabase silently substitutes the project Site URL, which would land an
# admin on app.<domain> with a dashboard session, no admin session, and nothing
# anywhere explaining why. That is the trade: no dashboard state to keep in step,
# at the cost of one-click sign-in.
#
# Consequence worth knowing before you deploy: AN ADMIN WHOSE ACCOUNT WAS CREATED
# THROUGH GOOGLE HAS NO PASSWORD, and this repo has no password-reset route at
# all. Set one for each admin from the Supabase dashboard (Authentication ->
# Users) before this goes live.
